Momentum, like the quantities velocity and force, has both direction and mag-
nitude. It is a vector quantity. Like velocity and force, momentum can be canceled. 
So, although the cannonball in the preceding example gains momentum when 
fired and the recoiling cannon gains momentum in the opposite direction, there is 
no gain in the cannon–cannonball system. The momenta (plural form of momen-
tum) of the cannonball and the cannon are equal in magnitude and opposite in 
direction.2 Therefore, as vectors, they add to zero for the system as a whole. Since 
no net external force acts on the system, there is no net impulse on the system and 
no net change in momentum. You can see that, if no net force or net impulse acts on 
a system, the momentum of that system cannot change.

