If a projectile just above the resistance of the atmosphere is given a horizontal 
speed somewhat greater than 8 km/s, it will overshoot a circular path and trace 
an oval path called an ellipse. An ellipse is a specific curve: the closed path 
taken by a point that moves in such a way that the sum of its distances from 
two fixed points (called foci) is constant. For a satellite orbiting a planet, one 
focus is at the center of the planet; the other focus could be internal or external 
to the planet. An ellipse can be easily constructed by using a pair of tacks (one 

