At the microscopic level, living cells must contend with the fact that the growth 
of volume is faster than the growth of surface area. Cells obtain nourishment by 
diffusion through their surfaces. As cells grow, their surface area increases, but not 
fast enough to keep up with their increasing volume. For example, if the surface 
area increases by 4, the corresponding volume increases by 8. Eight times as much 
mass must be sustained by only 4 times as much nourishment. At some size, the 
surface area isn’t large enough to allow sufficient nutrients to pass into the cell, 
which places a limit on how large cells can grow. So cells divide, and there is life 
as we know it. That’s nice.

