Interestingly, very deep ponds and lakes are normally not ice covered even in the 
coldest of winters. This is because all the water must be cooled to 4°C before lower 
temperatures can be reached. For deep water, the winter is not long enough to re-
duce an entire pond to 4°C. Any 4°C water lies at the bottom. Because of water’s 
high specific heat capacity and poor ability to conduct heat, the bottom of a deep 
body of water in a cold region remains at a constant 4°C year-round. Fish should 
be glad that this is so.

