	 16.4	 Newton’s Law of Cooling
Left to themselves, objects hotter than their surroundings eventually cool to 
match the surrounding temperature. The rate of cooling depends on how much 
hotter the object is than its surroundings. A hot apple pie will cool more each 
minute if it is put in a cold freezer than if it is left on the kitchen table. That’s 
because, in the freezer, the temperature difference between the pie and its sur-
roundings is greater. Similarly, the rate at which a warm house leaks internal 
energy to the cold outdoors depends on the difference between the inside and 
outside temperatures.

