	 16.6	 Climate Change
An important credo is “You can never change only one thing.” Change one thing, 
and you change another. A slightly higher Earth temperature means slightly 
warmer oceans, which means rising sea levels and changes in weather and storm 
patterns. The consensus among scientists is that Earth’s climate has gotten too 
warm too fast, and continues to do so. This phenomenon is called global warming 
or its result—climate change.

Weather is the state of the atmosphere at a particular time and place—its 
temperature, humidity, pressure, precipitation, wind, and clouds. Climate is the 
weather pattern over broader regions and longer times. One hot summer or a single 
hurricane is not evidence of global warming or climate change. Global warming 
and climate change are more than weather fluctuations, which come and go. Sci-
entific consensus is that climate change is here.



