How this will play out is not known. At one extreme, corrections can be made 
and life will be fine for Earth’s inhabitants. At the other extreme, we are reminded 
of the planet Venus, which in earlier times likely had a climate somewhat similar to 
Earth’s, until a runaway greenhouse effect led to its atmosphere today: 96% CO2 
with an average surface temperature of 460°C. Venus is the hottest planet in the 
solar system. We certainly don’t want to follow its course. It may take vigilance, 
not just luck, for Earth to remain pleasant and habitable for centuries to come.

One thing we don’t know is the long-term effect of disease-causing microbes and 
parasites when their natural habitats shift in basic ways. We also don’t know how 
agriculture will cope with soils drying out as water for irrigation gets scarcer. What 
we do know is that climate models indicate that ecosystems and human cultures 
worldwide will be severely disrupted due to glacial melting, sea-level rises, and more 
extreme weather events. We also know that energy consumption and changes in 
our atmosphere are related to population size. It is past time for humankind to 
address the question of continued growth. (Please take the time to read Appendix E, 
“Exponential Growth and Doubling Time”—very important material.)

