16.8 Controlling Heat Transfer
A nice way to review the methods of heat transfer is by considering a device that 
inhibits all three methods: the vacuum bottle. A vacuum bottle (known by the 
trademark name Thermos) consists of a double-walled glass container with a vac-
uum between the walls and a close-fitting stopper of cork or plastic. (There is 
usually an outer covering as well.) The glass surfaces that face each other are sil-
vered. Any liquid, hot or cold, in a vacuum bottle will remain close to its original 
temperature for many hours.

1. Heat transfer by conduction through the vacuum is impossible. Some heat 
escapes by conduction through the glass and stopper, but this is a slow pro-
cess because glass, plastic, and cork are poor conductors. F I G U R E  1 6 . 2 7

2. The vacuum has no fluid to convect, so there is no heat loss through the A Thermos bottle.
walls by convection.

3. Heat loss by radiation is reduced by the silvered surfaces of the walls, which 
reflect heat wavelengths back into the bottle.



