Standing Waves
If we tie a rope to a wall and shake the free end up and down, we produce a train 
of waves in the rope. The wall is too rigid to shake, so the waves are reflected back 
along the rope. By shaking the rope just right, we can cause the incident and re-
flected waves to form a standing wave, where parts of the rope, called the nodes,
appear to be standing still. Nodes are the regions of minimal or zero displacement, 
with minimal or zero energy. Antinodes (not labeled in Figure 19.12), in contrast, 
are the regions of maximum displacement and maximum energy. You can hold 
your fingers just over and under the nodes and the rope doesn’t touch them. Other 
parts of the rope, especially the antinodes, would make contact with your fingers. 
Antinodes occur halfway between nodes.

