The Doppler effect also occurs for light. When a light source approaches, there 
is an increase in its measured frequency; when it recedes, there is a decrease in its 
frequency. An increase in frequency is called a blue shift because the increase is 
toward the high-frequency (or blue) end of the color spectrum. A decrease in fre-
quency is called a red shift, referring to a shift toward the lower-frequency (or red) 
end of the color spectrum. Distant galaxies, for example, show a red shift in the 
light they emit. A measurement of this shift permits a calculation of their speeds 
of recession. A rapidly spinning star shows red-shifted light from the side turning 
away from us and blue-shifted light from the side turning toward us. This enables 
astronomers to calculate the star’s spin rate.

