	 19.8	 Shock Waves
A speedboat knifing through the water generates a two-dimensional bow wave. 
A supersonic aircraft similarly generates a three-dimensional shock wave. Just 
as a bow wave is produced by overlapping circles that form a V, a shock wave is 
produced by overlapping spheres that form a cone. And, just as the bow wave of a 
speedboat spreads until it reaches the shore of a lake, the conical wave generated by 
a supersonic craft spreads until it reaches the ground.

The bow wave of a speedboat that passes by can splash and douse you if you 
are at the water’s edge. In a sense, you can say that you are hit by a “water boom.” 
