We don’t hear a sonic boom from slower-than-sound, or subsonic, aircraft 
because the sound waves that reach our ears are perceived as one continuous tone. 
Only when the aircraft moves faster than sound do the waves overlap to reach the 
listener in a single burst. The sudden increase in pressure is much the same in effect 
as the sudden expansion of air produced by an explosion. Both processes direct a 
burst of high-pressure air to the listener. The ear is hard pressed to distinguish 
between the high pressure from an explosion and the high pressure from many 
overlapping waves.

