Physiological hearing damage begins at exposure to 85 decibels, with the 
degree of damage depending on the length of the exposure and on frequency 
characteristics. Damage from loud sounds can be temporary or permanent, 
depending on whether the nerve ending on the organ of Corti, the receptor 
organ in the inner ear, is impaired or destroyed. A single burst of sound can 
produce vibrations in the organ that are intense enough to tear it apart. Less 
intense, but severe, noise can interfere with cellular processes in the organ and 
cause its eventual breakdown. Unfortunately, the cells of the organ do not 
regenerate.

