	 21.5	 Musical Instruments
Conventional musical instruments can be grouped into three classes: those in 
which the sound is produced by vibrating strings, those in which the sound is 
produced by vibrating air columns, and those in which the sound is produced by 
percussion—as with the vibrating of a two-dimensional surface.

In a stringed instrument, the vibration of the strings is transferred to a 
sounding board and then to the air, but with low efficiency. To compensate for 
this, orchestras have relatively large string sections. A smaller number of the 
high-efficiency wind instruments sufficiently balances a much larger number 
of violins.

In a wind instrument, the sound is a vibration of an air column in the in-
strument. There are various ways to set air columns into vibration. In brass in-
struments, such as trumpets, French horns, and trombones, air is blown into the 
instrument’s mouthpiece at or near one end of the tube and exits at the other 
end. Vibrations of the player’s lips interact with standing waves that are set up by 

acoustic energy reflected within the instrument 
