Van de Graaff Generator
A common laboratory device for producing high voltages and 
creating static electricity is the Van de Graaff generator, invent-
ed by American physicist Robert J. Van de Graaff in 1931 to 
supply the high voltage needed for early particle accelerators. 
These accelerators were known as atom smashers because they 
accelerated subatomic particles to very high speeds and then 
“smashed” them into the target atoms. The resulting collisions 
could knock protons and neutrons out of atomic nuclei and 
create high-energy radiation such as X-rays and gamma rays. 
The ability to create these high-energy collisions is essential for 
particle and nuclear physics.

