The term magnetism comes from the name Magnesia, a coastal district of an-
cient Thessaly in Greece, where unusual stones were found by the Greeks more 
than 2000 years ago. These stones, called lodestones, had the intriguing property of 
attracting pieces of iron. Magnets were first fashioned into compasses and used for 
navigation by the Chinese in the 12th century.  

In the 16th century, William Gilbert, Queen Elizabeth’s physician, made arti-
ficial magnets by rubbing pieces of iron against lodestone, and he suggested that 
a compass always points north and south because Earth has magnetic properties. 
Later, in 1750, John Michell, an English physicist and astronomer, found that 
magnetic forces decrease with distance from magnetic sources, and his results were 
