	 27.8	 Why Clouds Are White
Water droplets are considerably larger than individual molecules and they behave 
differently. Rather than the scattering that occurs with tiny particles, water drop-
lets primarily refract and reflect light of all colors about equally. The result is a 
white cloud.

Each droplet absorbs a small fraction of the light that hits it, so if you have 
enough droplets, a lot of absorption occurs. Very little light gets through a really 
big cloud, which is why it looks dark. A cloud can also be darkened if it is in the 
shadow of another cloud. A further increase in the size of the droplets causes them 
to fall as raindrops, and we have rain.

