Chromatic aberration is the result of light of different colors having different 
speeds and hence different refractions in the lens (Figure 28.54). In a simple lens 
(as in a prism), different colors of light do not come to focus in the same place. 
Achromatic lenses, which combine simple lenses of different kinds of glass, correct 
this defect. (Interestingly, Isaac Newton replaced the objective lens in a telescope 
with a parabolic mirror to avoid chromatic aberration.) F i g u r e  2 8 . 5 4

