Light that is produced as a result of high temperature has the property of incan-
descence (from a Latin word meaning “to grow hot”). It can have a reddish tint, 
as from the heating element of a toaster, or a bluish tint, as from a particularly 
hot star. Or it can be white, as from the familiar incandescent lamp. What sets 
incandescent light apart from the light of a neon tube or mercury-vapor lamp is SCREENCAST: Emission of Light



