30.9 Lasers
The phenomena of excitation, fluorescence, and phosphorescence underlie the 
operation of a most intriguing instrument, the laser (light amplification by stim-
ulated emission of radiation).5 Although the first laser was conceived in 1958 
and actually built in 1960, the concept of stimulated emission was predicted by 

SCREENCAST: Laser Light
Albert Einstein in 1917. To understand how a laser operates, we must first discuss 
coherent light.

Light emitted by a common lamp is incoherent; that is, photons of many fre-
quencies and in many phases of vibration are emitted. The light is as incoherent 
as the footsteps on an auditorium floor when a mob of people are chaotically 
rushing about. Incoherent light is chaotic. A beam of incoherent light spreads out 
after a short distance, becoming wider and wider and less intense with increased 
distance.

