The Twin Trip
A dramatic illustration of time dilation is provided by identical twins, one an as-
tronaut who takes a high-speed round-trip journey in the galaxy while the other 
stays home on Earth. When the traveling twin returns, he is younger than the 
stay-at-home twin. How much younger depends on the relative speeds involved. 
If the traveling twin maintains a speed of 50% of the speed of light for 1 year (ac-
cording to clocks aboard the spaceship), 1.15 years will have elapsed on Earth. If 
the traveling twin maintains a speed of 87% of the speed of light for a year, then 
2 years will have elapsed on Earth. At 99.5% of the speed of light, 10 Earth years 
would pass in one spaceship year. At this speed, the traveling twin would age a 
single year while the stay-at-home twin would age 10 years.

