Graphing with Conceptual Physics

You may develop basic graphing skills in the laboratory part of this course. 
The labs “Sonic Ranger” and “Go! Go! Go!” introduce you to motion-graph-

ing concepts. “Motivating the Moving Man” uses a computer simulation that 
generates motion graphs. “Force Mirror,” like “Sonic Ranger,” involves sensor-
generated graphs with force sensors rather than motion sensors. “Totally Stressed 
Out” requires manual graphing of Hooke’s law data. The lab “The Weight” places 
emphasis on the interpretation of the slope of a plot. In “Water Waves in an 
Electric Sink” and “High Quiet Low Loud,” you’ll see how graphs can be used to 
represent waves and sounds.

You may also learn in the lab part of your Conceptual Physics course that com-
puters can graph data for you. You are not being lazy when you graph your data 
with a software program. Instead of investing time and energy scaling the axes 
and plotting points, you spend your time and energy investigating the meaning 
of the graph, a high level of thinking!

